**The `SecurityFilterChain` — an ordered chain of servlet filters.** Spring Security is, at its core, a `FilterChainProxy` registered as a single servlet filter that delegates to an **ordered list** of security filters. Each request passes through them *before* it ever reaches the `DispatcherServlet`. The order matters: exceptions are translated, then the request is authenticated, then authorization is enforced.

```java
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
@EnableMethodSecurity                       // turns on @PreAuthorize / @PostAuthorize
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http, JwtAuthenticationFilter jwtFilter)
            throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())                         // stateless JWT API
            .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(STATELESS))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/v1/auth/**").permitAll()   // login is open
                .anyRequest().authenticated())                    // everything else needs a token
            // place our JWT filter BEFORE the username/password filter
            .addFilterBefore(jwtFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class);
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

**JWT authentication flow.** For a stateless API there is no HTTP session — identity travels in the token on every request:

1. Client sends `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>`.
2. `JwtAuthenticationFilter` extracts the token from the header.
3. It validates the **signature** (with the server's public/secret key) and the **expiry** (`exp` claim).
4. It reads the claims (`sub`, `roles`) and builds an `Authentication` object with the authorities.
5. It stores that `Authentication` in the `SecurityContextHolder` — now the request is "logged in" for its lifetime.

```java
@Component
public class JwtAuthenticationFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    private final JwtDecoder jwtDecoder;    // verifies signature + expiry

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res,
                                    FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String header = req.getHeader("Authorization");
        if (header != null && header.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
            try {
                Jwt jwt = jwtDecoder.decode(header.substring(7));          // validate + decode
                List<GrantedAuthority> authorities =
                    ((List<String>) jwt.getClaim("roles")).stream()
                        .map(r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + r))
                        .collect(toList());
                var auth = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(
                        jwt.getSubject(), null, authorities);
                SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(auth); // "logged in"
            } catch (JwtException e) {
                res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED);         // bad/expired token
                return;
            }
        }
        chain.doFilter(req, res);   // continue toward DispatcherServlet
    }
}
```

**`@PreAuthorize` — method-level authorization.** With `@EnableMethodSecurity`, Spring wraps secured beans in an AOP proxy that evaluates a SpEL expression against the current `Authentication` **before** the method runs. If it fails, an `AccessDeniedException` becomes a `403`.

```java
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('TRADER')")                       // only TRADER may execute
public TradeResult execute(TradeRequest req) { ... }

@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")                        // only ADMIN may list all
public List<Trade> findAll() { ... }

@PreAuthorize("hasRole('VIEWER') and #ownerId == authentication.name")  // own data only
public List<Trade> findMine(String ownerId) { ... }
```

**`WebClient` for inter-service calls — propagating identity.** When the trade service calls the settlement service, the downstream call must carry an identity too. Either forward the incoming JWT, or (for background work) obtain a service token via the client-credentials grant using `ServerOAuth2AuthorizedClientManager`.

```java
@Bean
WebClient settlementClient(ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository regs,
                           ReactiveOAuth2AuthorizedClientService clients) {
    var manager = new AuthorizedClientServiceReactiveOAuth2AuthorizedClientManager(regs, clients);
    var oauth = new ServerOAuth2AuthorizedClientExchangeFilterFunction(manager);
    oauth.setDefaultClientRegistrationId("settlement-service");   // client-credentials grant
    return WebClient.builder()
            .baseUrl("http://settlement-service")
            .filter(oauth)                                        // auto-attaches Bearer token
            .build();
}
```

